imud NEWS — user-visible changes
================================

Newest first. This file records notable, user-visible changes per release. The
per-package changelogs under /usr/share/doc/<pkg>/changelog.gz hold the
packaging-level record, and the git history holds the full detail.


1.7
---
* Fixed: `imud-cal accel` printed the wrong physical instructions for all six
  positions, so an operator following them measured the wrong thing. imud's
  board frame has **Z pointing down**, meaning the axis pointing *up* reads
  +g — but the prompts said things like "+Z up (flat, normal side up)", and
  flat component-side up puts +Z *down*. The Z pair was inverted and the X and
  Y pairs were swapped with each other and inverted. The fit then divided by a
  negative or near-zero half-range and silently fell back to `scale = 1.0`
  with no warning, so the tool printed a clean-looking result and offered to
  save it. **If you have run `imud-cal accel` and followed the printed
  instructions, your accelerometer calibration almost certainly does nothing**
  — an identity scale and zero offset are what cal.json already defaults to,
  so the practical effect is that the daemon reported `FLAG_ACCEL_CAL` for a
  calibration that was not there. Worse outcomes were reachable: on real
  hardware a near-zero half-range can land just above the old 0.1 m/s^2
  threshold and produce a scale of up to ~98x, which warned but was still
  offered for saving. Re-run `imud-cal accel` with the corrected prompts.
  The six positions are now named the same way `imud-imutest` names them
  (flat component-side up, flat upside down, nose down, nose up, starboard
  side down, port side down), each prompt states the expected reading before
  you take it, and a geometrically impossible reading now names the offending
  position and exits without writing anything instead of silently fitting an
  identity. The position table and the fit moved to `src/cal_math.c` so both
  are covered by `test_cal_math`, including a regression case built from the
  exact readings the old instructions produced.
* New tool `imud-imutest` (in the imud-utils package): validates a sensor
  driver against real hardware and writes a Markdown report to attach to an
  issue. Ten of imud's twelve drivers have never run on physical silicon and
  are flagged `experimental`; their register maps are datasheet-checked and
  their encode/decode is unit-tested against a mock I²C bus, but nothing until
  now covered timing, real output rate, FIFO and interrupt behaviour, or
  whether the chip-to-board axis remap is correct. A passive phase checks all
  of that automatically — including a deliberate FIFO overflow, `seq`
  continuity, the -1-means-I²C-error contract, `chip_ts` wrap handling, and a
  sweep that re-initialises the part at every advertised full scale — and
  three guided phases walk the operator through the six-face, rotation and
  magnetometer-spin checks that actually prove the axis conventions. The spin
  phase cross-checks the magnetometer's heading against the gyro's Z integral,
  which is what catches the most common magnetometer-driver defect. Refuses to
  run alongside the daemon, since both would drain the same FIFO. A clean
  report is what clears an `experimental` flag: see ROADMAP §1.
* New drivers `mpu9250` / `mpu9255` (IMU) and `ak8963` (magnetometer), for the
  widely-deployed InvenSense/TDK MPU-925x 9-axis breakouts, by user request.
  All three are `experimental`. The magnetometer sits behind the IMU's I²C
  bypass at 0x0C, the same arrangement as the ICM-20948 and its AK09916, and
  the driver applies the AK8963's factory fuse-ROM sensitivity correction.
  Note the AK8963 is *rotated* relative to the host gyro/accel, unlike the
  AK09916 — see the header comment in `src/drivers/ak8963.c`. `probe()`
  rejects the relabelled MPU-6500s that are common in this market by name,
  rather than letting the failure surface later as an unexplained I²C error.
  Three of imud's defaults do not suit this part (FIFO watermark, ODR grid,
  and `mekf_accel_noise`) — see the fitting notes in manual §5.
* Wire format v17 (packet grows 260 → 276 bytes). Four new MEKF diagnostic
  fields. Two report how hard the filter is having to distrust its own
  sensors: `innov_weight` (EMA of the Huber weight applied to accepted
  updates; 1.0 = never capped) and `innov_reject` (EMA of the fraction of
  updates thrown out by the innovation gate). Two report whether the noise
  model itself is right: `nis_accel` and `nis_mag`, rolling averages of the
  normalised innovation squared d²/dof, where 1.0 means the filter's
  covariance is consistent with the innovations it actually sees and higher
  means it is over-confident. The NIS pair is accumulated before the Huber
  cap and includes gate-rejected updates, so — unlike `innov_weight`, which
  saturates once the cap engages — it keeps climbing as the model gets
  worse. Exposed via libimud, the Python client, and the Prometheus
  (`imud_nis_accel_ratio`, `imud_nis_mag_ratio`) and InfluxDB bridges. The
  daemon, all five bridges, and imud-mon validate the wire version and must
  be upgraded together; libimud consumers do not need recompiling.
* Fusion: the MEKF error state grows from 6 to 9 states with the addition of a
  Gauss-Markov wave-acceleration component, and the daemon now models the
  seaway's disturbance of the gravity measurement instead of pretending it is
  white sensor noise. Two new hot-reloadable keys tune it, `mekf_wave_accel`
  (steady-state sigma in m/s^2, default 0.8) and `mekf_wave_accel_tau_s`
  (correlation time in seconds, default 0.5); setting either to 0 disables the
  state and gives back the previous 6-state filter exactly. There is no wire
  change -- the new states are appended, so the covariance and gyro-bias
  variance fields keep their meaning and layout.

  Wave-orbital acceleration is correlated over roughly a second, so at 833 Hz
  the filter was being told it had 833 independent measurements of gravity per
  second when it had closer to one per wave. Modelling the correlation makes
  repeated samples correctly stop adding information. Measured over the 12-seed
  wave benchmark against 1.6: accelerometer NIS 19.3/25.2 -> 1.01/0.69 (1.0 =
  the noise model matches reality), covariance consistency NEES 18.3/7.8 ->
  3.47/0.99, attitude RMS 5.65 -> 4.45 deg (3-D) and unchanged at 2.31 deg
  (yaw-only, the marine default), heading RMS 3.07 -> 0.83 and 1.96 -> 1.02
  deg. Both the Huber innovation cap and the gross-outlier gate now sit
  completely idle in an ordinary seaway. This closes the long-standing
  "measurement model is over-confident" item (ROADMAP 10.1/10.5).

  `mekf_accel_noise` is unchanged and should now be understood as describing
  sensor noise only: rough weather is `mekf_wave_accel`'s job. `imud-cal
  fit-ra` replays a capture twice -- once as configured, once with the wave
  state forced off -- and prints suggested values for both new keys alongside
  the disturbance it measured.
* Fixed: the initial attitude alignment averaged a hardcoded ~1 second of
  sensor data. That is about a fifth of a typical roll period, so a daemon
  started underway aligned to an arbitrary point in the wave cycle, and the
  resulting tilt error was baked permanently into the magnetic reference's dip.
  Measured over the wave benchmark, attitude RMS in the marine default: 47.7
  degrees at a 1 s window against 2.19 degrees at 5 s, flat beyond. The window
  is now `align_window_sec` in `[calibration]`, default 5 s. Installs that
  start up at a mooring were unaffected; anything starting up in a seaway was
  not.
* New `[fusion]` key `mekf_mag_dip_sigma_deg` (default 1.0), the uncertainty of
  the magnetic reference's dip angle. It only affects 3-D vector fusion
  (`mag_yaw_only = false`), where the field's dip constrains roll and pitch and
  a wrong dip reference therefore biases them. That error cannot be learned out
  at sea, so it is now admitted into the covariance instead: the magnetometer
  update gains a rank-1 anisotropic noise term along the one direction a dip
  error acts on. Covariance consistency (NEES-strict) in 3-D mode improves 12.8
  to 5.7 with attitude and heading both marginally better; the marine yaw-only
  default is bit-for-bit unchanged. Note this lowers the `nis_mag` wire field in
  3-D mode (0.52 to 0.31) by design -- the dip channel is deliberately trusted
  less. Set it to 0 if a position source supplies WMM field invariants, which
  removes the dip error at the source and is the better answer where available.
* Docs: the wire spec, libimud spec and manual now state explicitly that the
  Euler attitude fields (`pitch`, `roll`, `yaw`, `heading_deg`,
  `rate_of_turn`) are ZYX intrinsic angles and are singular at pitch +/-90 deg
  by construction -- approaching vertical, roll and yaw stop being separately
  determined and swing wildly. Consumers that can encounter steep pitch
  (gimbals, tilting camera rigs, aircraft) must read `quat_*`, which is never
  singular, as is the covariance in `cov[9]`. Nothing changed in the packet:
  the quaternion has always been there. Surface-vessel consumers are
  unaffected and can keep using the Euler fields.
* Flag bit 6 (`FLAG_MOTION` / `IMUD_FLAG_MOTION`) is formally retired. It was
  defined but never set in any released version, and everything it could have
  reported is already on the wire at higher fidelity: `accel_quiescence` as a
  continuous float, and `FLAG_ENGINE_ON`. The define stays in all headers so
  existing code compiles; the bit will never be set and will not be reused.
  Previously the wire spec described it as "significant motion detected",
  which implied a feature that did not exist.
* Fixed: `imud-signalk`'s `dest_addr` accepted a numeric IPv4 address only,
  while every other bridge resolved its destination with getaddrinfo(3) and
  accepted hostnames. It now uses the same shared helper, so a hostname, an
  IPv6 address, or a broadcast address all work. The bridge's systemd unit
  gains AF_INET6 to match -- without it a hostname resolving to IPv6 would
  fail at socket() under the unit's sandbox. The daemon's own [output] and
  [highrate] destinations remain numeric-only by design: there is no DNS in
  the daemon's send path.
* Earlier 1.7 notes attributed the 3-D-mode covariance inconsistency to the
  2-D swing-circle magnetometer calibration. That was wrong -- the cause was
  the alignment window above -- and the documentation has been corrected.
* Fixed: the MEKF declared its innovation covariance singular using an
  absolute determinant threshold (1e-12). That matrix carries physical units,
  and for the gravity update its determinant sits near 1e-13 at perfectly
  ordinary conditioning -- so the accelerometer update was being silently
  skipped whenever the filter converged, discarding 87% of accelerometer
  updates in the wave benchmark and leaving attitude uncertainty pinned at
  whatever value kept the determinant above the threshold instead of letting
  it converge. The health and consistency metrics were fed only by the
  surviving updates. The test is now relative to the size of the matrix, which
  is what "singular" actually means. Numbers recorded in earlier documentation
  for filter accuracy and consistency were measured with this bug present and
  are not comparable; the current figures are in docs/math.md 4.7.
* Fixed: `imud-cal fit-ra` estimated the residual correlation time by dividing
  by the IMU sample period, but its autocorrelation is computed over the
  samples that survive the accelerometer skip band -- typically 3 to 10 times
  further apart. Reported correlation times were low by that factor.
* Fixed: `imud-cal fit-ra` duplicated the filter's innovation and d-squared
  computation rather than calling it, and the two had drifted apart (different
  singularity thresholds). It now calls the filter's own code, so the offline
  report and the live `nis_accel` field cannot disagree.
* Fusion: the MEKF's gross-outlier rejection threshold moves from 9x to 25x
  the chi-squared innovation gate. At 9x it was discarding 26% of the wave
  benchmark's accelerometer updates -- ordinary wave-orbital motion, not
  outliers -- which starved the filter; the resulting drift produced larger
  innovations, which tripped the gate more often still. Widening it improves
  every measured quantity in both magnetometer modes at no cost: attitude RMS
  6.85 deg -> 5.65 deg (3-D) and 3.57 deg -> 2.31 deg (yaw-only, the marine
  default), heading RMS 4.27 -> 3.07 and 3.10 -> 1.96, and covariance
  consistency NEES 28.9 -> 18.3 and 21.1 -> 7.8. It also collapses the
  benchmark's worst-case spread (3-D 13.68 -> 7.02, yaw-only 9.59 -> 2.34),
  which had previously been put down to scenario luck. The Huber cap that
  bounds each update's influence is unchanged, as is `mekf_accel_noise`.
* Fixed: the update-gate health and consistency metrics (`innov_weight`,
  `innov_reject`, `nis_accel`) averaged with a per-update gain derived from
  the IMU sample rate, but the accelerometer skip band discards 85-95% of
  samples in a seaway, so their true time constant was around ten minutes
  rather than the intended 30 s -- long enough that they reported their
  initial values instead of the data. The gain is now derived from elapsed
  time, which is correct at any update rate.
* Fusion: the error-state reset Jacobian G = I − ½[δθ]× is now applied to the
  covariance after each correction is injected. Measured over the wave
  benchmark this improves yaw-only attitude RMS 4.10° → 3.57° and covariance
  consistency (NEES 32.4 → 28.4), with 3-D attitude unchanged. (Both figures
  predate the rejection-gate change above.)
* New `imud-cal fit-ra` mode: replays a rough-water .imucap capture through
  the filter and reports the gravity-direction residual, its correlation
  time, the innovation-distance distribution against the gates, and the mean
  NIS -- the same statistic the daemon publishes live. Unlike the other
  modes it writes nothing: the value it comments on is filter tuning in
  imud.conf, not a sensor property. Requires --from FILE and a magnetometer
  calibration.
* Config: the four MEKF noise densities (`mekf_gyro_noise`, `mekf_gyro_bias`,
  `mekf_accel_noise`, `mekf_mag_noise`) are now rejected at load if zero or
  negative. Each becomes a variance in a denominator; `mekf_accel_noise = 0`
  gave a Kalman gain of exactly 1, snapping attitude onto every raw
  accelerometer sample.
* Fixed: the packet fuzz target's seed corpus still held a v14/260-byte
  frame, which packet_ok() rejects on size and version before reaching any
  decode logic -- so the nightly fuzz run had been starting that target from
  an empty effective corpus since the wire moved to v17. The seed is now
  generated from packet_build() itself (`make fuzz-seeds`) and test_packet
  asserts it still matches the wire the code implements, so the next wire
  change fails a test instead of silently degrading the fuzzer.
* Documented: `mekf_accel_noise` should not be hand-tuned upward to "allow
  for waves". The default is a sharp local optimum for the marine
  configuration and the range 0.003-0.03 is markedly worse than either side
  of it. See man 5 imud.conf and docs/math.md 4.7 for the full sweep.
* Engine-vibration detector: the EMA time constant is now derived from the
  sample rate instead of being a fixed per-sample constant, so it is a true
  ~1 s regardless of FIFO burst depth (previously it sped up as bursts got
  deeper). The threshold gained hysteresis — it releases at 70% of the assert
  level — so a signal sitting on the boundary no longer chatters `Ra_scale`,
  the accel skip band, the log, and the `ENGINE_ON` wire flag.
* Fixed: a config reload (SIGHUP) while the engine was running left the accel
  skip band narrow with the noise inflation still applied, and stayed that
  way until the engine next stopped.
* Fixed: a config reload did not recompute the m_ref adaptation rate, so
  changing `mag_odr_hz` left it running at the old rate indefinitely.
* Config, `[mount]`: an unrecognised `preset` name is now a fatal error
  instead of a warning. It previously left the mount marked as configured
  with whatever angles happened to be in place, so a typo silently applied a
  wrong rotation to every sample. `rotation_euler_deg` now requires exactly
  three values; a short array was previously accepted with the missing angles
  left at their defaults.
* Config, `[mount]`: new `rotation_matrix` key accepts the board→body
  rotation directly as 9 row-major values, validated at load against
  RᵀR = I and det(R) = +1 (a reflection — typically a sign-flipped axis —
  is rejected, which an orthogonality-only check would not catch).
* The rough-sea wave benchmark now averages over a fixed set of 12 seeds
  instead of asserting on one. The single seed it used was unrepresentative:
  it scored 5.15° where the mean across seeds is 6.71° and the worst draw
  13.78°, which both flattered the filter and made unrelated changes look
  like regressions.

1.6.2
-----
* Internal refactor: duplicated code across the daemon, drivers, and
  bridges consolidated into shared helpers — no behavior, wire, config,
  or log-output change. The five bridge daemons now share their startup
  and main-loop scaffolding (src/bridge.c + src/sdnotify.c), all I2C
  drivers share the register-access helpers (src/drivers/i2c_io.h), and
  the WMM declination/field-reference derivation is a single function
  used by both the static-position and GPS paths. Net −835 lines.
* New test suite (test_bridge) covers the shared bridge scaffolding:
  emit-tick timing math, CLI handling, config load/reload, systemd
  notification delivery, and stream reconnect behavior.


1.6.1
-----
* Bridge output posture — daemon on, outputs off. Every bridge
  (imud-signalk, imud-mqtt, imud-influxdb, imud-mavlink, imud-prometheus)
  now separates the daemon-level `enabled` from its per-output enables. A
  stock installed config enables the daemon but ships every output OFF, so
  the service starts, stays healthy under systemd, and reads imud's stream
  while emitting nothing until you turn on the output(s) you want. This
  matches how the main imud daemon already treats its section enables.
  - imud-signalk: new `udp_enabled` gates the UDP delta output, independent
    of the existing `tcp_enabled` (enable either or both). BEHAVIOR CHANGE:
    UDP used to send whenever the daemon ran; an existing config that relied
    on that must now add `udp_enabled = true`.
  - imud-influxdb: new `udp_enabled` and `http_enabled` (both may run at
    once) replace the `transport = "udp"|"http"` selector. `transport` is
    DEPRECATED but still honored — when neither enable is set it is mapped
    to the matching output with a one-time warning, so existing configs keep
    working.
  - imud-mqtt: new `broker_enabled` gates the broker connection/publish;
    keeps a fresh install from repeatedly dialing an unconfigured broker.
  - imud-prometheus: new `http_enabled` gates the /metrics listener.
  - imud-mavlink: unchanged keys (it already had per-transport enables); the
    stock config now ships `enabled = true` like the others.
* Fix: a bridge with `enabled = false` no longer crash-loops under systemd.
  It now signals readiness before exiting cleanly, so the Type=notify unit
  records a successful start and stops (inactive/dead) instead of tripping
  the start-protocol-failure + Restart=on-failure loop. Upgraded systems
  keep their existing /etc/imud/*.conf (dpkg conffiles); bridges left at the
  old `enabled = false` default now exit cleanly rather than looping.


1.6
---
* TCP outputs. Every consumer-facing stream can now serve TCP clients
  alongside (not replacing) its existing transport, because that is how the
  consuming software actually connects — chartplotter apps, signalk-server,
  and ground stations are TCP clients first:
  - [nmea] tcp_enabled: NMEA-over-TCP listener (port 10110) — OpenCPN,
    Navionics, and phone/tablet nav apps connect to tcp://HOST:10110.
  - [stream] tcp_enabled: the loss-free framed binary stream over TCP
    (port 10112) — remote imud-mon, remote bridges, laptop consumers.
  - imud-signalk tcp_enabled: newline-framed deltas on a TCP listener
    (port 10113) for a Signal K server TCP data connection.
  - imud-mavlink tcp_enabled: a TCP listener (port 5760) that
    QGroundControl / Mission Planner connect to directly.
  Each listener accepts up to 8 clients; a slow client drops
  payloads/frames, never stalls the daemon. All tcp_* keys are [restart];
  bind defaults to 0.0.0.0 (set tcp_bind_addr = 127.0.0.1 to stay local).
* BEHAVIOR CHANGE — default output posture. A stock daemon (no config file,
  or one that doesn't say otherwise) now enables ONLY the local AF_UNIX
  [stream] socket: nmea.enabled flipped true -> false, stream.enabled
  flipped false -> true. Bridges and libimud consumers work out of the box,
  and nothing touches the network until an output is explicitly enabled.
  Existing installs that relied on the built-in NMEA-UDP-on default must
  set [nmea] enabled = true (the shipped /etc/imud/imud.conf has carried
  this stream-only posture since 1.5, so installs using it are unaffected).
* libimud: new imud_connect_tcp(host, port) — connect to a daemon's
  [stream] TCP listener; the remote equivalent of imud_connect_stream, same
  framed-read path. Additive (symbol version IMUD_1, SONAME stays
  libimud.so.0). Python: ImudClient.connect_tcp(host, port) /
  ImudClient(tcp=(host, port)), and imud_client.py --tcp HOST[:PORT].
* New companion repository: imud-arduino
  (https://github.com/richcreations/imud-arduino) — an Arduino/ESP32 client
  library (ImudClient) that receives the binary stream over the [stream]
  TCP listener or high-rate UDP, for gauges, displays, and remotes built on
  microcontrollers.
* New shared TCP-server core (src/netserv.c) used by the daemon and both
  bridges: non-blocking accept, broadcast-only (clients are never read),
  the stream thread's proven slow-client policy, single owning thread per
  listener; covered by the new test_netserv suite plus TCP paths in
  test_stream and test_libimud.


1.5.1
-----
* Fix two C11 data races in the 1.5 release (neither is caught by the
  normal or ASan/UBSan test runs):
  - Hot-reload / live navigation values: the fusion and reader threads now
    read the daemon config through a mutex-guarded snapshot, and SIGHUP
    republishes hot fields to each consumer (atomic output rates, a locked
    snapshot for the status thread) instead of mutating a shared struct
    that other threads read concurrently. As a side effect, [fusion]
    heave_tau_s / wave_tau_s / mag_yaw_only now actually take effect on
    SIGHUP (previously they were dropped by the reload).
  - Stream shutdown: the final packet to stream subscribers is now sent by
    the stream thread itself as it exits, so the subscriber list has a
    single owner and is never traversed by the main thread concurrently.
* Reject non-finite and malformed numbers in gpsd / Signal K position input
  (NaN, Inf, truncated/garbage values, out-of-range latitude/longitude) so
  hostile or buggy upstream data can no longer poison declination, the
  magnetic reference, or the centripetal correction.
* Log pthread errors using the function's return code (strerror(rc)) rather
  than errno, which POSIX threads calls do not set.
* New test_concurrency suite drives the reader/fusion/output threads under a
  config-reload storm and stream-shutdown churn; CI runs it under
  ThreadSanitizer.


1.5 (unreleased)
----------------
* Capture & replay — the black box: [capture] records every raw sensor
  sample (pre-mount, pre-calibration) to rotating .imucap files; the sim
  driver replays them through the full pipeline (`imud --replay FILE`, or
  [device] sim_file/sim_loop/sim_speed), and imud-status reports the
  recording. Crash-safe, bounded-disk, and documented in docs/capture.md.
  Fixed along the way (found by the new end-to-end replay test): the sim
  driver's pitch axis was mirrored between its accel/mag and gyro outputs,
  the mag reader treated "data not ready" as data (re-fusing a stale
  sample), and two rotations within the same second could reuse a capture
  filename and clobber the earlier file (names are now kept monotonic).
* Per-unit sensor characterization: `imud-cal characterize --from <capture>`
  measures gyro/accel noise density and gyro bias instability (Allan
  variance) into cal.json's "noise" section — a record of what your silicon
  actually does. The numbers are informational: the filter keeps its tuned
  [fusion] mekf_* values, which are held above the raw sensor floor on
  purpose (the gyro terms are the filter's process noise, not a sensor
  readout), so nothing is auto-tuned from them.
* Gyro temperature compensation: `imud-cal fit-temp --from <capture>` fits
  per-axis bias/temperature coefficients; the daemon subtracts the thermal
  term before fusion.
* Fixed: the IMU die temperature was always reported as 25.0 degC. The
  ISM330DHCX and LSM6DSO drivers never enabled FIFO temperature batching, so
  the temperature never updated from its placeholder — in every release to
  date. This affected everything that surfaces it: the binary packet's temp_c,
  imud-mon, the MQTT/InfluxDB/Prometheus telemetry (imud_temperature_celsius
  and friends), and libimud's imud_data_t.temp_c. Temperature now reads real
  values; this is also what makes the new fit-temp mode usable at all, and it
  means the daemon's gyro thermal compensation was previously a no-op.
* Uncalibrated startup (no cal.json): the filter now takes its initial heading
  from the uncalibrated magnetometer — rough, but a real bearing — instead of
  assuming the bow points north. It also no longer counts thousands of phantom
  FIFO overflows (visible in imud-status) while waiting for the first
  magnetometer sample.
* Config values too long for their field are now reported ("value too long —
  truncated") instead of being silently shortened, so an over-long socket path
  can no longer bind somewhere you did not ask for.
* Debian packages: a debian/ build producing ten .debs (imud, imud-utils,
  libimud0, libimud-dev, the five bridges, imud-wmm-data), built for amd64
  and arm64 in CI; version tags now draft a GitHub release with the .debs
  and source tarball attached. imud-mon moved to the new imud-utils package
  (`make install-utils`).
* The shipped reference config now enables only the local AF_UNIX stream
  socket (the input the bridges and libimud consumers read); enable [nmea]
  there for chartplotters. The built-in defaults with no config file are
  unchanged (NMEA on). Fresh installs no longer broadcast UDP out of the box.
* SIGHUP now also refreshes the filter's WMM magnetic-field reference when
  lat/lon or wmm_file change; previously only the declination updated and
  the field-magnitude invariants kept their startup values until restart.
* Experimental-driver fixes (still unvalidated on real silicon): ak09916 no
  longer counts "data not ready"/magnetic overflow as I2C errors, which
  could trigger spurious chip resets after 10 slow reads in a row; the
  icm42688p's hardware timestamps were scaled 25x too coarse and wrapped
  every ~1 s (20-bit counter) — per-driver tick rates and counter
  unwrapping fix the emitted ts_wall_ns/ts_tai_ns for that chip.
* CI: ThreadSanitizer job (all cross-thread stop flags are now C11 _Atomic)
  and libFuzzer harnesses over the four untrusted-input parsers (config,
  gpsd/Signal K JSON, wire packet, capture reader). The packet fuzzer
  immediately found — and this release fixes — a client-side hang:
  imud_true_heading()'s degree normalization looped effectively forever on
  a crafted packet carrying Inf or huge floats, so a hostile UDP datagram
  could wedge any libimud/imud_client.h consumer. Out-of-range values now
  return the -1.0 sentinel.


1.4.1
-----
* World Magnetic Model data split out of the imud package: WMM.COF now
  installs to /usr/share/imud/ via its own target (`make install-wmm-data`,
  packaged as imud-wmm-data, versioned by model epoch — 2025.0) so a new
  five-year model can ship without a daemon release. The [position]
  wmm_file default is now empty = auto-resolve: an operator-supplied
  /etc/imud/WMM.COF (where 1.4-and-earlier installs put it) is preferred,
  then the packaged /usr/share/imud/WMM.COF.


1.4
---
* Sea state: significant wave height (Hs = 4σ(heave)), mean zero-crossing
  wave period, and the vessel's roll and pitch periods and significant
  single amplitudes (2σ), computed from the heave/attitude oscillations
  over a configurable window ([fusion] wave_tau_s, default 120 s). New
  WAVE_VALID flag gates the outputs until the statistics settle.
* Compass health: two always-on diagnostics — mag_anomaly (fractional field-
  magnitude deviation; detects magnetic interference and iron-cal drift) and
  mag_residual (mean absolute heading innovation; calibration health) —
  computed inside the filter, including from rejected measurements.
* New ENGINE_ON flag exports the engine-vibration detector's state; the MQTT
  bridge publishes it as a Home Assistant binary_sensor (engine-hours
  automations).
* Binary stream packet version 14 (260 bytes): the eight fields above are
  appended before the CRC. Wire-pinned consumers must be rebuilt; libimud
  applications keep working without recompiling.
* New bridge: imud-prometheus — serves the fused state as Prometheus
  /metrics gauges (base SI units, flag bits as 0/1 gauges, alerting-ready).
  The first bridge built purely on libimud's ABI-stable interface.
* imud_client.h (the single-header C client) is deprecated: use libimud.
  It remains in the source tree for vendoring and wire-pinned internals but
  is no longer installed by `make install`.
* CI: builds and tests now also run on arm64 (the Pi's architecture) and
  under AddressSanitizer/UBSan; fixed the 1.3 release-verification checks
  (hardening-check fortify false positive on libimud.so, versioned-symbol
  handling in the ABI check).


1.3
---
* New: libimud, an ABI-stable client shared library (libimud.so.0) with a
  public imud.h and pkg-config metadata. Applications link -limud and keep
  working across imud upgrades without recompiling — data is exposed through
  an append-only struct behind an opaque handle, over the local stream socket
  or the UDP stream. See man 3 libimud.
* The four bridges now link libimud (their duplicated stream-socket code is
  gone). No wire-format change: packet version stays 12, and existing
  imud_client.h / imud_client.py consumers are unaffected.


1.2
---
* Binary stream packet version 12 (228 bytes): export more of the fusion state
  imud already computes — estimated gyro bias (x/y/z) and its variance, heave
  rate (vertical velocity), and an accel-quiescence (platform-disturbance)
  metric — plus a new HEAVE_VALID flag that signals when the heave estimate has
  settled. Stream consumers must be rebuilt against the new packet layout.
* Bridges surface the new data where each protocol has a home for it:
  imud-influxdb logs the full v12 diagnostic set (gyro bias + variance, heave
  rate, quiescence, and a heave_valid field); imud-mqtt adds a heave-rate topic;
  and the user-facing bridges (Signal K, MQTT) now withhold heave until the
  estimator settles, so no startup transient is published. imud-mavlink is
  unchanged (ATTITUDE already carries attitude + gyro body rates).


1.1
---
* Optional bridge daemons — each shipped as its own package with its own config
  file, systemd service, man pages, and documentation:
    - imud-signalk   Signal K deltas over UDP
    - imud-mqtt      MQTT topics + Home Assistant discovery (via libmosquitto)
    - imud-influxdb  InfluxDB line protocol over UDP or HTTP (pure C)
    - imud-mavlink   MAVLink v1/v2 (HEARTBEAT/ATTITUDE) over UDP and/or serial
* Binary stream packet version 11: adds heave and magnetic-declination fields.
* Heave estimator and a rough-sea fusion revision.
* Level-gated logging (debug/info/warn/error), journald-aware.
* Documentation brought to GNU + Debian conventions: AUTHORS/NEWS/INSTALL/
  CONTRIBUTING, per-package copyright and changelog, and per-package docs
  installed under /usr/share/doc/.


1.0
---
* Initial release. Quaternion Multiplicative EKF attitude/heading from an I²C
  IMU + magnetometer; NMEA 0183, high-rate binary UDP, and a local AF_UNIX
  subscription stream; World Magnetic Model true heading; gyro / accelerometer /
  magnetometer calibration tools (imud-cal); status and monitoring tools
  (imud-status, imud-mon); a hardened systemd service.
